[stderr] ## Objective
[stderr] 
[stderr] Use F001–F010 to create the canonical specification beneath
[stderr] `projects/<project-slug>/specification/`. Consolidate the brief, feature,
[stderr] technical, experience, and delivery documents into a coherent set of documents
[stderr] that any later author can use without rediscovering scope. Assign stable IDs
[stderr] such as `REQ-0001`, `NFR-0001`, `DEC-0001`, `RISK-0001`, and `GATE-0001`.
[stderr] 
[stderr] This must work for a game, app, operating-system component, document set,
[stderr] service, desktop product, TV product, or any other selected product category.
[stderr] Model target-neutral requirements separately from target-specific adaptations.
[stderr] For a large product, create a specification index plus bounded domain chapters
[stderr] (for example combat, quests, progression, social, economy, tools, or platform
[stderr] delivery), rather than requiring one document to contain every detail.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The canonical specification must prove that it understood what kind of product
[stderr] it is building. It must convert the discovery map into an appropriate complete
[stderr] domain specification before work is decomposed. For example, this may be an
[stderr] original story/world/character and game-rules bible for an RPG, a language and
[stderr] extension platform specification for an IDE, or a system-service and security
[stderr] model for an operating system. No category is privileged; use the disciplines
[stderr] discovered from the brief.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Acceptance criteria
[stderr] 
[stderr] - `requirements.md` and `requirements.json` list every functional, quality,
[stderr]   accessibility, privacy, platform, release, and content requirement with ID,
[stderr]   priority, source, rationale, target applicability, verification method, and
[stderr]   status.
[stderr] - A traceability matrix maps every requirement to journeys, components,
[stderr]   implementation-contract IDs, implementation runbook IDs when assigned, and
[stderr]   final evidence.
[stderr] - A decision log records owner, due point, alternatives, default behaviour, and
[stderr]   consequence for every unresolved decision; no decision is silently invented.
[stderr] - A platform appendix records each selected platform's input, capability,
[stderr]   policy, distribution, accessibility, performance, and parity differences.
[stderr] - The specification index records chapter IDs, ownership, dependencies, and
[stderr]   completion state so later tasks can expand it in small, resumable waves.
[stderr] - A domain-completeness review explicitly checks every discovered system family
[stderr]   has a requirements chapter, entities/rules, cross-domain dependencies,
[stderr]   authoring model where needed, and a release-scope decision.
[stderr] - Original-design and reference boundaries remain explicit in every document.
