[stderr] playwright_spec:
[stderr] ---
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Objective
[stderr] 
[stderr] Create `projects/<project-slug>/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json` and
[stderr] its readable Markdown companion. It is a persistent allocation ledger, not a
[stderr] one-shot list: partition the currently available `IMP-*` catalogue into ordered,
[stderr] small authoring batches and retain queued domain-expansion work. Each batch
[stderr] will later be a normal Supervisor runbook that writes a bounded set of final
[stderr] implementation runbooks.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Use IDs `B0001`, `B0002`, … for authoring batches and reserve IDs `R0001`,
[stderr] `R0002`, … for generated implementation runbooks. Limit a batch to at most 7
[stderr] R-series contracts and reduce that limit when context, coupling, or a platform
[stderr] domain requires it. This is the scaling mechanism: a thousand implementation
[stderr] runbooks become many independent, resumable authoring tasks.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Acceptance criteria
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Every available `IMP-*` record belongs to exactly one batch or has a
[stderr]   documented human gate/deferment; queued requirements identify their future
[stderr]   expansion chapter. Every allocated `R-*` ID is unique and immutable.
[stderr] - Batch ordering respects the catalogue dependency graph and identifies batches
[stderr]   safe to author in parallel versus those requiring a preceding decision.
[stderr] - Each batch has a context packet listing only the relevant specification,
[stderr]   catalogue records, terminology, target constraints, and required templates.
[stderr] - No authoring batch is assigned more than seven R-series contracts; a smaller
[stderr]   batch is required when its contracts are tightly coupled or context-heavy.
[stderr] - The manifest records expected runbook count, IDs, dependencies, output paths,
[stderr]   acceptance checks, checkpoint cadence, recovery instructions, and an explicit
[stderr]   asset assessment for every allocated R contract. The assessment records
[stderr]   `asset_impact` as either `required` or `not_applicable`; asset-required
[stderr]   contracts reserve stable, product-specific `asset_ids` and identify their
[stderr]   asset brief/purpose before a B-series writer is assigned.
[stderr] - Batches preserve end-to-end vertical slices as well as foundation work, so a
[stderr]   large product can be implemented and reviewed incrementally.
[stderr] - The ledger contains monotonic next-ID counters and wave state, so a resumed
[stderr]   dispatcher never renumbers accepted B-series or R-series work.
