task_id: F010
sequence: 10
title: Finalize the specification-to-runbook handoff inputs
browser_impact: not_applicable
playwright_spec:
---

## Objective

Create `projects/<project-slug>/planning/10-authoring-handoff.md` with the
execution order, dependencies, decision gates, assumptions, risks, and
instructions for the batch authoring system that follows in F011–F016.

## Acceptance criteria

- The handoff identifies what will and will not be built and preserves original-design boundaries.
- It states safe agent configuration, evidence locations, required human/external approval, and stop conditions.
- It confirms the catalogue is ready for batch authoring; no implementation or release action is performed.
---
task_id: F011
sequence: 11
title: Build the canonical specification and requirements traceability system
browser_impact: not_applicable
playwright_spec:
---

## Objective

Use F001–F010 to create the canonical specification beneath
`projects/<project-slug>/specification/`. Consolidate the brief, feature,
technical, experience, and delivery documents into a coherent set of documents
that any later author can use without rediscovering scope. Assign stable IDs
such as `REQ-0001`, `NFR-0001`, `DEC-0001`, `RISK-0001`, and `GATE-0001`.

This must work for a game, app, operating-system component, document set,
service, desktop product, TV product, or any other selected product category.
Model target-neutral requirements separately from target-specific adaptations.
For a large product, create a specification index plus bounded domain chapters
(for example combat, quests, progression, social, economy, tools, or platform
delivery), rather than requiring one document to contain every detail.

The canonical specification must prove that it understood what kind of product
it is building. It must convert the discovery map into an appropriate complete
domain specification before work is decomposed. For example, this may be an
original story/world/character and game-rules bible for an RPG, a language and
extension platform specification for an IDE, or a system-service and security
model for an operating system. No category is privileged; use the disciplines
discovered from the brief.

## Acceptance criteria

- `requirements.md` and `requirements.json` list every functional, quality,
  accessibility, privacy, platform, release, and content requirement with ID,
  priority, source, rationale, target applicability, verification method, and
  status.
- A traceability matrix maps every requirement to journeys, components,
  implementation-contract IDs, implementation runbook IDs when assigned, and
  final evidence.
- A decision log records owner, due point, alternatives, default behaviour, and
  consequence for every unresolved decision; no decision is silently invented.
- A platform appendix records each selected platform's input, capability,
  policy, distribution, accessibility, performance, and parity differences.
- The specification index records chapter IDs, ownership, dependencies, and
  completion state so later tasks can expand it in small, resumable waves.
- A domain-completeness review explicitly checks every discovered system family
  has a requirements chapter, entities/rules, cross-domain dependencies,
  authoring model where needed, and a release-scope decision.
- Original-design and reference boundaries remain explicit in every document.
---
task_id: F012
sequence: 12
title: Build the implementation catalogue and dependency graph
browser_impact: not_applicable
playwright_spec:
---

## Objective

Create `projects/<project-slug>/planning/implementation-catalogue-index.md` and
`implementation-catalogue-index.json` from the canonical specification.
Decompose the first bounded domain chapter (at most 250 implementation
contracts) into atomic implementation contracts,
using stable IDs such as `IMP-0001`, and create a domain-expansion queue for
the remaining chapters. Do not write the final R-series runbooks yet.

An implementation contract is the smallest independently verifiable slice that
can be safely handed to an implementation agent. It may be code, content,
configuration, research with an explicit output, test automation, migration,
documentation, build/release work, or platform certification work.

Derive contracts from the complete domain specification, including product
content, tooling, operations, and quality work as first-class implementation
work where relevant. An RPG might yield story, character, quest, combat,
balancing, localisation, and content-tool contracts; an IDE might yield parser,
language-service, debugger, extension-host, workspace, and source-control
contracts; an operating system might yield kernel, driver, identity, process,
storage, installer, and system-settings contracts. The mechanism must derive,
not assume, the appropriate set for any product.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every record includes title, domain, target(s), requirement IDs, bounded
  outcome, prerequisites, files/data/API ownership, forbidden scope,
  verification, human/external gate, and estimated authoring complexity.
- The index covers foundation, data, domain logic, UI/UX, content, input,
  accessibility, security, privacy, performance, observability, tests,
  operations, documentation, delivery, and platform-specific work when in
  scope. Each expansion task adds at most 250 contracts in one bounded domain
  shard rather than making an
  unbounded one-shot request.
- Dependencies form an acyclic graph; cross-cutting prerequisites are explicit
  and no task has ambiguous ownership.
- The catalogue distinguishes shared work from each platform adapter and avoids
  a false assumption that every target has the same UI or delivery path.
- The index reports every `REQ-*`/`NFR-*` as covered by a current contract,
  queued for a named domain expansion, intentionally deferred, gated, or
  rejected with a recorded reason.
---
task_id: F013
sequence: 13
title: Plan scalable runbook-authoring batches
browser_impact: not_applicable
playwright_spec:
---

## Objective

Create `projects/<project-slug>/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json` and
its readable Markdown companion. It is a persistent allocation ledger, not a
one-shot list: partition the currently available `IMP-*` catalogue into ordered,
small authoring batches and retain queued domain-expansion work. Each batch
will later be a normal Supervisor runbook that writes a bounded set of final
implementation runbooks.

Use IDs `B0001`, `B0002`, … for authoring batches and reserve IDs `R0001`,
`R0002`, … for generated implementation runbooks. Limit a batch to at most 7
R-series contracts and reduce that limit when context, coupling, or a platform
domain requires it. This is the scaling mechanism: a thousand implementation
runbooks become many independent, resumable authoring tasks.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every available `IMP-*` record belongs to exactly one batch or has a
  documented human gate/deferment; queued requirements identify their future
  expansion chapter. Every allocated `R-*` ID is unique and immutable.
- Batch ordering respects the catalogue dependency graph and identifies batches
  safe to author in parallel versus those requiring a preceding decision.
- Each batch has a context packet listing only the relevant specification,
  catalogue records, terminology, target constraints, and required templates.
- No authoring batch is assigned more than seven R-series contracts; a smaller
  batch is required when its contracts are tightly coupled or context-heavy.
- The manifest records expected runbook count, IDs, dependencies, output paths,
  acceptance checks, checkpoint cadence, recovery instructions, and an explicit
  asset assessment for every allocated R contract. The assessment records
  `asset_impact` as either `required` or `not_applicable`; asset-required
  contracts reserve stable, product-specific `asset_ids` and identify their
  asset brief/purpose before a B-series writer is assigned.
- Batches preserve end-to-end vertical slices as well as foundation work, so a
  large product can be implemented and reviewed incrementally.
- The ledger contains monotonic next-ID counters and wave state, so a resumed
  dispatcher never renumbers accepted B-series or R-series work.
---
task_id: F014
sequence: 14
title: Generate the bounded implementation-runbook authoring collection
browser_impact: not_applicable
playwright_spec:
---

## Objective

Generate only the bootstrap wave of ordinary Supervisor runbooks in
`projects/<project-slug>/authoring-runbooks/`: a bounded catalogue-expansion
task followed by a dispatcher task. The dispatcher allocates at most ten
authoring B-series tasks per wave, creates one successor dispatcher, and stops.
Each authoring task instructs Codex to create only its assigned R-series
implementation runbooks under
`projects/<project-slug>/runbooks/`, then validate their metadata and update the
manifest. The successor dispatcher repeats this process until no queued
catalogue chapter or unassigned implementation contract remains. Generate an
`INITIAL.md` context document in `authoring-runbooks/`
that points to the canonical `PROJECT_BRIEF.md` and specification.

Register the generated authoring collection with the source collection by
writing `runbooks/.supervisor-children/<project-slug>.json` containing
`{"runbooks_dir": "../projects/<project-slug>/authoring-runbooks"}`. Also
write `authoring-runbooks/.supervisor-children/implementation.json` containing
`{"runbooks_dir": "../runbooks"}`. These are explicit child-collection
registrations; `supervisor-run --run-all` follows them automatically after the
parent collection is accepted.

Every dispatcher first expands at most one queued specification/catalogue domain
chapter (maximum 250 `IMP-*` contracts), validates its coverage, then allocates
ready contracts. If any queued chapter or ready contract remains, it creates no
more than ten authoring tasks and exactly one successor dispatcher. If none
remains, it creates the final audit task instead. This is a durable loop, not a
recursive prompt or an unbounded single run.

Every generated R-series runbook must use the standard Supervisor front matter
and contain: precise objective; dependencies; relevant requirement IDs; owned
files/data/interfaces; required behaviour; non-goals; platform differences;
verification commands/evidence; and observable acceptance criteria. It must be
small enough for one implementation task, not a vague epic.

Every generated R-series runbook must also include a detailed asset assessment.
Its front matter must always declare both fields below, even when no assets are
needed:

```yaml
asset_impact: required # or not_applicable
asset_ids: village_gate_main,village_gate_construction # blank only when not_applicable
```

For `asset_impact: required`, the B-series writer must reserve stable,
product-specific asset IDs (never generic names such as `image1`), provide an
`asset_brief` and `visual_style_version`, and include an `## Asset assessment`
section explaining the assets' purpose, where they appear, the required visual
states/variants, source-of-truth ownership, and original-art acceptance checks.
For `not_applicable`, that section must explain why the scope needs no generated
or source visual asset and explicitly prohibit incidental asset creation. This
keeps UI, illustration, icon, texture, sprite, audio-visual, and other product
asset work visible in the implementation plan instead of discovering it midway
through coding.

Each B-series authoring task may create at most seven R-series implementation
runbooks. It must stop after its assigned seven (or fewer), update the manifest,
and leave remaining work for the next bounded batch.

## Acceptance criteria

- The initial collection contains only a bounded bootstrap wave; each
  dispatcher expands at most one 250-contract domain shard, creates at most ten
  new B-series authoring tasks plus one successor, then exits. It never attempts
  to materialise the whole game plan.
- There is exactly one valid B-series authoring runbook per allocated manifest
  batch, and it creates no more than seven assigned R-series runbooks.
- B-series runbooks never overwrite an existing R-series contract with a
  conflicting ID; they stop and report mismatches.
- Every generated B-series runbook includes its context packet, output list,
  validation procedure, checkpoint/update procedure, and a clear stop condition.
- Both child-collection registration files exist, contain valid JSON with a
  relative `runbooks_dir`, and point to the generated authoring and
  implementation collections respectively.
- R-series contracts use stable IDs and parse with the Supervisor runbook
  loader; their declared dependencies match the manifest, every contract has
  explicit `asset_impact` and `asset_ids` metadata, and asset-required
  contracts have non-empty stable IDs.
- The collection contains a final audit B-series task after all authoring
  batches and catalogue expansions are complete, rather than pretending that
  thousands of contracts can be reviewed in one generation step.
---
task_id: F015
sequence: 15
title: Validate generated authoring batches and implementation contracts
browser_impact: not_applicable
playwright_spec:
---

## Objective

Create `projects/<project-slug>/planning/runbook-generation-quality-gate.md`
and validation instructions/scripts that can be run after every B-series wave.
Validate the authoring collection itself and the R-series contracts it produces.
The validation is structural and traceability-based; it must not claim the
product has been implemented or released.

## Acceptance criteria

- Validation checks required front matter, unique IDs, sequence ordering,
  requirement links, dependency closure, output ownership, and unsupported
  circular dependencies.
- Validation checks every R-series contract has an explicit asset assessment:
  `asset_impact` is `required` or `not_applicable`, `asset_ids` is present,
  asset-required contracts reserve non-empty unique stable IDs and an asset
  brief/style version, and non-asset contracts do not silently introduce asset
  work. It reports missing, duplicate, ambiguous, or unowned asset IDs.
- It detects duplicate browser test paths and confirms browser-impact metadata
  only where browser behaviour is actually in scope.
- It reports catalogue/manifest/specification coverage counts and identifies
  every missing, duplicate, blocked, or unverified contract by ID.
- It fails the quality gate when a discovered domain family has no specification
  chapter, content/authoring approach where applicable, implementation
  contracts, or traceable verification. It checks category-appropriate
  completeness rather than applying a fixed checklist to every product.
- It includes recovery: how to rerun one failed B-series authoring task, repair
  a bounded R-series range, and resume the next dispatcher wave without
  regenerating accepted work.
- Human gates, paid services, credentials, legal/compliance decisions, device
  access, and store/certification actions remain explicit review points.
---
task_id: F016
sequence: 16
title: Publish the project-scoped runbook factory handoff
browser_impact: not_applicable
playwright_spec:
---

## Objective

Create `projects/<project-slug>/README.md` as the human handoff for this
project-scoped factory. Explain the three levels of work: the source brief and
canonical specification; B-series authoring runbooks; and R-series
implementation runbooks. Document ordinary Supervisor commands for each level
and the chosen agent pipeline for the eventual implementation project.

## Acceptance criteria

- The README gives exact, project-relative commands to run the factory, then
  the B-series collection, then selected/all R-series implementation contracts.
- It names all generated directories, source-of-truth files, manifests,
  validation reports, evidence locations, and safe resume points.
- It explains how a large project scales: run a dispatcher wave, run the newly
  allocated B-series tasks, validate, and repeat until the final audit. Keep
  B-series batches bounded and add new waves for approved scope changes rather
  than mutating accepted contracts.
- It states that responsive web/PWA is included by default but all platform
  requirements come from the selected targets in the original brief.
- It preserves non-goals, original-design/reference boundaries, human gates,
  and the fact that authoring contracts is not implementation or release.
- It links the domain-discovery map and canonical specification so a future
  reviewer can see why the generated runbooks include the systems they do.

